// expect: refuse E_TypeMismatch
// An `if` condition is a Bool: the condition is unified with `Bool` at the
// `if`, so what it flows from learns it. `g`'s parameter reaches the
// condition and nothing else, so `g([1, 2])` is a list where a Bool stands
// and refuses at the argument. Until 2026-10-06 the judgment overwrote the
// condition node's own cell with `Bool` instead of unifying it, the
// parameter never learned it, and this program checked clean and ran to 1.
fn g(c) = if c { 1 } else { 2 }

fn main() = g([1, 2])
